<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarisation requires.

  Every one of these is a hole in the hardened runtime, so each is here for a stated
  reason rather than because a guide said so:

    allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      .NET compiles IL to machine code as it runs. Under the hardened runtime that is
      exactly what is forbidden, and without both of these the app dies at startup
      rather than misbehaving later.

    disable-library-validation
      Luma loads libvlc and its 339 plugins, which come out of VideoLAN's own release.
      Library validation demands that loaded code carry the same Team ID as the app;
      these never will. The alternative is re-signing all 340 of them with our own
      identity, which claims authorship of somebody else's binaries.

  Deliberately absent: everything to do with the App Sandbox. Luma is distributed
  directly, not through the App Store, and a sandboxed video player would have to ask
  permission for every folder a film might live in.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
